file=target/compiler-native-inline128.predeclared.md
1f1f87b02b4b899fdf994237ed173468608f0789c3d594fbd7cef0e8cadc7671  target/compiler-native-inline128.predeclared.md
# Producer inline128: predeclared follow-on protocol

**Preparation only. No construction, runtime observation or oracle has run.** The four-worker gate owns the compute slots until root explicitly releases one worker.

This experiment asks whether increasing the existing, rechecked producer inliner from 64 to 128 removes enough remaining call-boundary work to reduce the fixed compiler witness's whole runtime by at least 10%. It is one specified threshold comparison, with no threshold search or retries. The accepted inline64 result, discarded inline16 result and one instrumented attribution pair remain separate evidence; this experiment does not imply a backend floor.

## Fixed inputs

- Worktree base: `d4d6911a99fc9f3daa6c6994f2898a5ecc528ad8`.
- Computation and producer source: `26bf2c3c021dd79810a81fa1d9054d59f3768b06`; source tree `3431671024d0a550b3130f2401bde067026d9bd3`, library tree `e442fb3920db7eda2377f92fde0d92761f0562be`.
- Common emitter: decompressed directly from `a4fd685a9bb2e7736110a4ed2a43651dfe040d56:seed/lkjscript.gz`, never from the mutable main-worktree target. Its required SHA-256 is `4c4537ea7ccb31213f376d1402f619883eea6f8877fdb8b752b31df364099e09`, 20,627,356 bytes. The compressed archive must have SHA-256 `6c4b72837d4982c1c90c24ee8212c0934f6a5b836bb7d06194f5a894be3cf306`.
- Every measured output must equal the frozen original seed exactly: 16,228,028 bytes, SHA-256 `8ca3225bebb5738376479d7556cd6d88b666805391fd2477331523ee6c666132`.
- Both producer construction and measured execution use the same archived physical library and runtime, literal source argument `src/main.lkj`, and `--no-store`. Measured outputs always use `--inline-limit 0`.

## Sequence and decision

One common emitter constructs the two producer images, first at limit64 and then at limit128, with `--stats`. Each transformation must be accepted after checking, with zero rejected transformations. Construction costs, expanded-producer image sizes, RSS, stats and paired 1/5/15-minute load are separate from runtime observations.

The six uninstrumented executions run once each, in this fixed order:

| Run | Producer | Repetition within policy |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 64 | 1 |
| 2 | 128 | 1 |
| 3 | 128 | 2 |
| 4 | 64 | 2 |
| 5 | 64 | 3 |
| 6 | 128 | 3 |

Each output is retained at its own path and compared byte-for-byte against the original frozen seed. Empty stdout/stderr and exact digest/length are recorded. All runtime rows, including adverse load or slow observations, remain in the result.

The primary retention metric is the reduction in arithmetic mean GNU-time wall duration over all three runs per policy. The six displayed centisecond durations are compared as integers: candidate sum × 10 must be at most baseline sum × 9. CPU means are secondary descriptive evidence. No observation is selected or rerun.

Only if that condition is met does the candidate run the frozen original-IR optimizer oracle. The oracle script remains byte-identical to `26bf2c3c`; because it hardcodes optimized limit64, a predeclared compiler wrapper translates only the exact argument pair `--inline-limit 64` to `--inline-limit 128`. Zero-policy commands, test cases, mutation seed/count, jobs, interpretation and every other argument remain unchanged. The wrapper hash, per-invocation translation counts and actual arguments are retained. The frozen script's internal labels and log filenames may still say 64; their actual optimized compiler arguments are 128, while zero/default arguments and mutation seed 1701/count 128/jobs 1 are unchanged. The oracle's cost is recorded separately and is excluded from runtime means.

A miss discards inline128 and skips the oracle. A refusal, byte mismatch or failed oracle is retained as that outcome, without retry or policy adoption. A candidate that clears 10% and passes the oracle is reported for separate root review before any bootstrap, compiler or seed-policy change.

## Bounds, evidence and cleanup

The harness fixes one worker, a 4 GiB external address-space ceiling, 900 s per measured command and a 10 s termination grace. These are host experiment bounds, not language resource policy. It requires 3 GiB of free space before generating the owned fixture. File caches are not reset. No hash microbenchmark, profiler run, new Rust comparison or optimization implementation belongs to this campaign.

The harness is `bench/compiler_native_inline128.sh`. It refuses to overwrite evidence and records preparation status, per-phase argument lists, start/capture timestamps, loads, GNU-time measurements, stdout/stderr, statistics, output parity, the predeclared decision and final outcome. An interrupted supervisor leaves checkpoints for honest refusal/unknown accounting. Raw requests or binaries from other worktrees are never modified.

After independent acceptance, `--cleanup OWNED_DIRECTORY` checks the fixture's repository/owner markers and live-process references, removes only that owned tree, and verifies its absence separately. Archived source, both producers, all six outputs, decompressed seeds and any oracle store/key are temporary. Textual evidence remains. No main, seed, release, tag or push change is part of this experiment.

